France. He would finally win the Tour de France in 1980, after five second place finishes (and a sixth in 1982), and he also won the Vuelta a España in 1979. In 1985, Zoetemelk became the oldest ever winner of the World Professional Road Race. The Swedish team was unique in Olympic history, consisting of four brothers, the Pettersson’s – " [:a {:href "/athletes/14284"} "Erik"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/14285"} "Gösta"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/14286"} "Sture"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/14287"} "Tomas"] ". World Champions in 1967, 1968, and 1979, they would later change their name to Fåglum, in honor of the village in which they grew up."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Bib"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Netherlands"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {} "2-07:49.06"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "9"} [:a {:href "/athletes/14133"} "Fedor den Hertog"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/15152"} "Jan Krekels"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/16132"} "René Pijnen"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/17280"} "Joop Zoetemelk"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Sweden"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]] [:td {} "2-09:26.60"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "9"} [:a {:href "/athletes/14284"} "Erik Pettersson"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/14285"} "Gösta Pettersson"] " • " [:a {:href "/at